Java applications are organized into types such as classes, interfaces, enums, and records. A class can contain fields, constructors, methods, nested types, and other members.

A simple Java class can be written as:

```java
public class Employee {
    private String name;

    public void displayName() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
```

The class declaration defines the type, the field represents state, and the method represents behavior.

The traditional entry point for a standalone Java application is the `main` method. A commonly used form is `public static void main(String[] args)`.

`public` makes the method accessible to the application launcher. `static` allows it to be invoked without creating an instance of the class. `void` indicates that it does not return a value, and `String[] args` receives command-line arguments.

For example:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println("Application started");
}
```

A statement represents an instruction that can be executed, while a block is a group of statements enclosed in curly braces. Blocks are commonly used with classes, methods, constructors, conditional statements, and loops.

For example:

```java
if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Adult");
}
```

The `if` statement controls execution, while the statements inside `{}` form its block.

Java has eight primitive data types: `byte`, `short`, `int`, `long`, `float`, `double`, `char`, and `boolean`.

The integer types represent signed integer values with different widths. `float` and `double` represent floating-point values. `char` represents a UTF-16 code unit, and `boolean` represents either `true` or `false`.

Example:

```java
int age = 25;
long population = 8000000000L;
double salary = 50000.75;
char grade = 'A';
boolean active = true;
```

Reference types represent objects and arrays rather than primitive values. A reference variable contains a reference to an object or can contain `null` when it does not currently refer to an object.

Examples of reference types include classes, interfaces, arrays, enums, and records.

```java
Employee employee = new Employee();
String name = "Java";
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};
```

Java variables are commonly categorized according to where they are declared. A local variable is declared inside a method, constructor, or block. An instance variable is a field associated with an object. A static variable is a class-level field associated with the class rather than an individual instance.

```java
class Employee {
    static String company = "ABC";
    String name;

    void work() {
        int hours = 8;
    }
}
```

Here, `company` is static, `name` is an instance variable, and `hours` is a local variable.

A narrowing numeric conversion converts a value to a type with a smaller range or precision. Such conversions generally require an explicit cast because information may be lost.

```java
double price = 99.99;
int value = (int) price;
```

The resulting integer is `99` because the fractional part is discarded during this conversion.

Arithmetic operators are used for numeric calculations. Java provides operators such as `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and `%`. Assignment operators assign values to variables and include `=`, `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, and `/=`.

```java
int a = 10;
int b = 3;

int sum = a + b;
int remainder = a % b;
a += 5;
```

Conditional statements allow a program to choose different execution paths based on boolean expressions. An `if` statement executes its block when its condition is true. An `else` block provides an alternative path, while an `else-if` chain allows multiple conditions to be evaluated.

```java
if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Adult");
} else {
    System.out.println("Minor");
}
```

A switch statement selects an execution path based on the value of a selector expression. Java supports traditional switch statements and newer switch expressions.

A modern switch can use arrow labels:

```java
switch (day) {
    case 1 -> System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2 -> System.out.println("Tuesday");
    default -> System.out.println("Unknown");
}
```

Switch is useful when multiple branches depend on one selector value.

Loops repeatedly execute a block of code while a condition or iteration rule allows it. A `for` loop is commonly used when initialization, condition, and update can be expressed together. A `while` loop checks its condition before each iteration. A `do-while` loop executes the body before checking its condition, so the body executes at least once.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

The `break` statement terminates the nearest applicable loop or switch statement. The `continue` statement skips the remaining part of the current loop iteration and proceeds to the next iteration.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 5) {
        break;
    }
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

Here, the loop terminates when `i` becomes 5.

An array is an object that stores a fixed number of elements of a particular component type. Array indexes start at zero, and the length of an array is fixed when the array is created.

```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};

System.out.println(numbers[0]);
System.out.println(numbers.length);
```

The first element is at index 0 and the last element is at index `length - 1`.

An array can be declared using either `type[] name` or `type name[]`, although the first style is generally preferred. Arrays can be initialized using an array initializer or by specifying a length with `new`.

```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};
int[] values = new int[5];
```

When an array is created, its elements receive the default value appropriate to the component type.

Java supports arrays whose elements are themselves arrays. This is commonly used to represent two-dimensional or multidimensional data.

```java
int[][] matrix = {
    {1, 2},
    {3, 4}
};

System.out.println(matrix[1][0]);
```

Java multidimensional arrays are technically arrays of arrays, which means rows can have different lengths.

Array indexes must be within the range from zero through `length - 1`. Accessing an index outside this range results in an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};
System.out.println(numbers[5]);
```

The valid indexes are 0, 1, and 2, so index 5 is invalid.

String immutability means that an existing String object's character sequence cannot be modified. Operations that appear to change a String instead produce another String value.

```java
String name = "Java";
name.concat(" Programming");

System.out.println(name);
```

The original value remains `Java` because the returned String was not assigned back to `name`.

Java maintains a pool of canonical String instances associated with string literals. When identical string literals are used, they can refer to the same pooled String object.

```java
String a = "Java";
String b = "Java";

System.out.println(a == b);
```

The expression can evaluate to `true` because both variables can refer to the same pooled object. This should not be confused with content comparison, for which `equals()` should normally be used.

StringBuilder and StringBuffer provide mutable sequences of characters. They are useful when an application performs repeated modifications to text.

StringBuilder is not synchronized and is generally preferred for ordinary single-threaded string construction. StringBuffer provides synchronized methods and is an older class retained for compatibility and specific use cases where that synchronization behavior is required.

```java
StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();
builder.append("Hello");
builder.append(" World");
```

Unlike String, the existing StringBuilder object can be modified.

For object references, `==` checks reference identity, while `equals()` checks logical equality according to the class implementation. String overrides `equals()` to compare its character contents.

```java
String a = new String("Java");
String b = new String("Java");

System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(a.equals(b));
```

The first comparison is false because the references identify different objects, while the second is true because their contents are equal.

A method is a named declaration that defines behavior associated with a class or object. A method can accept parameters, perform operations, and optionally return a value.

```java
public int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

Here, `int` is the return type, `add` is the method name, and `a` and `b` are parameters.

Method overloading allows methods with the same name to have different parameter lists. The compiler determines which applicable overload should be invoked based on the method arguments.

```java
int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

int add(int a, int b, int c) {
    return a + b + c;
}
```

Changing only the return type does not create a valid overload.

Java is always pass-by-value. When a primitive is passed to a method, its value is copied. When an object is passed, the value copied is the reference to that object.

This means a method can modify the state of an object through the copied reference, but assigning a different object to the parameter does not change the caller's reference.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
}

void changeName(Employee employee) {
    employee.name = "John";
}
```

A class is a reference type that defines the structure and behavior of objects. A class can contain fields, methods, constructors, nested types, and other members.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int age;

    void work() {
        System.out.println("Employee is working");
    }
}
```

The fields represent possible state, while the method represents behavior.

An object is a runtime instance of a class. Objects have identity and state, and they can provide behavior through their methods.

```java
Employee employee = new Employee();
employee.name = "John";
employee.age = 30;
```

`new Employee()` creates an Employee object, while `employee` is the reference variable used to access it.

A constructor is a special declaration used during object creation to initialize a new object. A constructor has the same name as its class and does not declare a return type.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;

    Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

When `new Employee("John")` is evaluated, the matching constructor is invoked as part of object creation.

A class can define multiple constructors with different parameter lists. This is called constructor overloading and allows objects to be initialized using different sets of inputs.

```java
class Employee {
    Employee() {
    }

    Employee(String name) {
    }

    Employee(String name, int age) {
    }
}
```

Constructors are not inherited by subclasses. However, when a subclass object is created, the subclass constructor must participate in superclass initialization. A constructor can explicitly invoke a superclass constructor using `super(...)`, or the compiler can insert an implicit invocation of the accessible no-argument superclass constructor when appropriate.

```java
class Animal {
    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal constructor");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        super();
        System.out.println("Dog constructor");
    }
}
```

The `this` keyword refers to the current object in an instance context. It is commonly used when a field and a parameter have the same name.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;

    Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

Here, `this.name` refers to the instance field, while `name` refers to the constructor parameter.

The `this(...)` form is used to invoke another constructor in the same class. It can be used to centralize initialization logic and avoid repeating constructor code.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int age;

    Employee() {
        this("Unknown", 0);
    }

    Employee(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}
```

A constructor invocation using `this(...)` must appear as the first statement of the constructor.

The `super` keyword is used within a subclass to refer to members of its immediate superclass. It can be used to access a superclass field, invoke a superclass method, or invoke a superclass constructor.

```java
class Animal {
    String name = "Animal";
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    String name = "Dog";

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(super.name);
    }
}
```

The first reference accesses the Dog field, while `super.name` accesses the inherited superclass field.

A static field belongs to the class rather than to a particular object instance. A single class variable is associated with the class, and instances can access it according to the normal accessibility rules.

```java
class Employee {
    static String company = "ABC";
    String name;
}

System.out.println(Employee.company);
```

The `company` field is class-level state rather than separate state for every Employee object.

A static method belongs to the class and can be invoked without creating an instance of that class.

```java
class Calculator {
    static int square(int number) {
        return number * number;
    }
}

int result = Calculator.square(5);
```

A static method does not have an implicit `this` reference, so it cannot directly access instance fields or instance methods.

A class can contain static initialization blocks that execute when the class is initialized. They are commonly used when class-level initialization requires more than a simple field initializer.

```java
class Configuration {
    static String environment;

    static {
        environment = "production";
    }
}
```

The JVM initializes a class before its static members are used in ways that require class initialization, subject to the Java Language Specification's initialization rules.

A final variable can be assigned only once. After a final variable has been initialized, it cannot be assigned another value.

```java
final int MAX_RETRIES = 3;
```

For a final reference variable, the reference cannot be reassigned, but the object referred to can still be mutable unless the object's own state is immutable or otherwise restricted.

```java
final List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("John");
```

The reference `names` cannot point to another List, but the existing List can be modified.

A final instance method cannot be overridden by a subclass. This allows a class to prevent subclasses from replacing a particular method implementation.

```java
class Parent {
    final void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}
```

A subclass can inherit the method but cannot provide an overriding implementation for it.

A final class cannot be extended by another class.

```java
final class Calculator {
}
```

A declaration such as `class AdvancedCalculator extends Calculator` would be invalid because Calculator is final.

The `private` modifier restricts direct access to the declaring top-level class or member-owning class. A private field or method cannot be accessed directly from unrelated classes.

```java
class Employee {
    private double salary;
}
```

Private members are commonly used when a class should control access to its internal state or implementation.

When no access modifier is specified for a top-level type or member, the declaration has package-private accessibility. It can be accessed by code in the same package, subject to the applicable language rules.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
}
```

The field `name` has package-private accessibility because no modifier is specified.

The `protected` modifier permits access from code in the same package. It also permits access from subclasses in other packages, subject to Java's specific protected-access rules.

```java
class Employee {
    protected double salary;
}
```

Protected access is often encountered in inheritance-based designs, although composition and interfaces are also common approaches to structuring Java applications.

The `public` modifier makes a declaration accessible wherever the declaring type and its enclosing context are accessible.

```java
public class Employee {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Working");
    }
}
```

Public members can form part of the externally visible API of a class or application component.

A package is a namespace used to organize related Java types. Packages help avoid naming conflicts and provide a boundary for package-private access.

A package declaration is normally placed at the beginning of a Java source file:

```java
package com.example.employee;

public class Employee {
}
```

An import declaration allows a Java source file to refer to accessible types using their simple names rather than their fully qualified names.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
```

Importing a package does not automatically import its subpackages. Java also supports static imports for referring directly to accessible static members.

A `try` block contains code that may throw an exception, while a matching `catch` block handles an exception of the specified type.

```java
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}
```

The catch parameter provides access to the exception object and its information.

The `finally` block is associated with a try statement and is commonly used for cleanup actions that should occur after the try/catch processing. Java's try-with-resources construct is generally preferred for automatically closing resources that implement `AutoCloseable`.

```java
try {
    // operation
} finally {
    // cleanup
}
```

The `throw` statement explicitly throws an exception object.

```java
throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid age");
```

The `throws` clause appears in a method or constructor declaration and indicates exceptions that the method may propagate to its caller.

```java
void readFile() throws IOException {
    // code
}
```

In short, `throw` is an executable statement, while `throws` is part of a declaration.

The `if` statement executes a block only when its condition evaluates to `true`. The condition must be a boolean expression in Java.

```java
int age = 20;

if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Eligible to vote");
}
```

If `age >= 18` evaluates to true, the message is printed. If it evaluates to false, Java skips the block and continues with the statement after it.

Java does not automatically convert numbers into boolean values. For example, `if (age)` is invalid because `age` is an integer rather than a boolean expression.

The `if-else` statement provides two possible execution paths. The `if` block executes when the condition is true; otherwise, the `else` block executes.

```java
int marks = 65;

if (marks >= 40) {
    System.out.println("Pass");
} else {
    System.out.println("Fail");
}
```

Exactly one of the two blocks is selected. This is useful when the program needs to handle both outcomes of a decision.

The `else` block does not have its own condition. It represents everything that remains after the `if` condition evaluates to false.

An `else-if` ladder is useful when there are multiple mutually exclusive conditions. Java checks the conditions from top to bottom and executes the first block whose condition is true.

```java
int marks = 82;

if (marks >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A+");
} else if (marks >= 75) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (marks >= 60) {
    System.out.println("B");
} else {
    System.out.println("C");
}
```

Once the `marks >= 75` condition is found to be true, Java executes that block and skips the remaining conditions. This means the order of conditions matters. A broad condition placed too early can prevent a later, more specific condition from ever being evaluated.

A nested `if` is an `if` statement placed inside another conditional block. It is useful when one decision depends on another decision.

```java
int age = 25;
boolean hasLicense = true;

if (age >= 18) {
    if (hasLicense) {
        System.out.println("Can drive");
    } else {
        System.out.println("License required");
    }
} else {
    System.out.println("Too young to drive");
}
```

The inner condition is checked only when execution reaches it. In this example, the license check is relevant only after the age requirement has been satisfied.

Nested conditions are sometimes the clearest representation of dependent decisions, but excessive nesting can make logic harder to read. In such cases, combining conditions or extracting a meaningful method can make the intent clearer.

The traditional `switch` statement selects a block based on the value of an expression. It is useful when several possible branches depend on the same value.

```java
int day = 2;

switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
        break;
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Wednesday");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Invalid day");
}
```

Java evaluates the switch expression and searches for a matching case. If no case matches, the `default` branch is used when it is present.

Traditional switch statements can work with integral types such as `byte`, `short`, `char`, and `int`, their corresponding wrapper types, `String`, and enum values. The exact set of supported forms depends on the Java version and language features being used.

In a traditional switch statement, execution does not automatically stop when a matching case finishes. If `break` is omitted, execution continues into the following cases. This behavior is called fall-through.

```java
int value = 1;

switch (value) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("One");
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Two");
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Three");
}
```

When `value` is 1, all three messages are printed because there is no `break` after case 1 or case 2.

Fall-through can be intentional when several cases should share behavior, but accidental fall-through is a common source of bugs in traditional switch code.

Modern Java provides switch expressions that can directly produce a value. The arrow syntax makes the intended result easier to see and does not have the traditional fall-through behavior.

```java
int day = 2;

String type = switch (day) {
    case 1, 7 -> "Weekend";
    case 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 -> "Weekday";
    default -> "Invalid";
};

System.out.println(type);
```

A switch expression is particularly useful when a decision determines the value assigned to a variable. When a case requires multiple statements, a block can be used together with `yield` to provide the value.

```java
String result = switch (day) {
    case 1 -> "Monday";
    case 2 -> {
        String message = "Tuesday";
        yield message;
    }
    default -> "Other";
};
```

The `for` loop combines initialization, a continuation condition, and an update expression in one structure.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

The initialization runs once before the first iteration. The condition is then evaluated before each iteration. If it is true, the body executes. After the body finishes, the update expression runs and the condition is checked again.

The three expressions are optional. For example, `for (;;) { }` is syntactically valid and creates an infinite loop unless the loop is terminated from inside its body.

The enhanced `for` loop, commonly called the for-each loop, provides a simple way to iterate through arrays and objects that implement `Iterable`.

```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};

for (int number : numbers) {
    System.out.println(number);
}
```

Java automatically obtains each element and assigns it to the loop variable. This avoids manually managing an index and often makes simple traversal easier to read.

The enhanced loop is not a replacement for every traditional loop. If the program needs the element index, needs to move through only part of an array, or needs more specialized traversal logic, a traditional loop may be more appropriate.

A `while` loop checks its condition before executing the body. Therefore, the body may execute zero times if the condition is false from the beginning.

```java
int count = 1;

while (count <= 5) {
    System.out.println(count);
    count++;
}
```

The loop continues while the condition remains true. Something inside the loop normally changes the state used by the condition so that the loop can eventually terminate.

A while loop is especially useful when the number of iterations is not known beforehand. For example, a program might continue reading input until it receives a particular command.

The `do-while` loop checks its condition after executing the body. This guarantees that the body runs at least once.

```java
int choice = 1;

do {
    System.out.println("Displaying menu");
    choice++;
} while (choice <= 3);
```

This makes `do-while` useful when the operation needs to happen before the program decides whether another iteration is required. Menu-driven programs are a common example because the menu needs to be displayed before the program can determine what should happen next.

The semicolon after the `while` condition is part of the syntax:

```java
do {
    // body
} while (condition);
```

A nested loop is a loop inside another loop. The inner loop normally completes its iterations for every iteration of the outer loop.

```java
for (int row = 1; row <= 3; row++) {
    for (int column = 1; column <= 3; column++) {
        System.out.println("Row: " + row + ", Column: " + column);
    }
}
```

Nested loops are common when working with two-dimensional arrays, matrices, tables, and pattern problems. If both loops execute approximately `n` times, the total number of inner executions can be approximately `n × n`, commonly expressed as O(n²).

The actual complexity depends on the loop bounds. An inner loop that executes a constant number of times has a different complexity from one whose number of iterations grows with the outer loop.

The `break` statement immediately terminates the nearest enclosing loop or switch statement. Execution continues with the first statement after that structure.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    if (i == 5) {
        break;
    }
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

When `i` becomes 5, the break statement terminates the loop. As a result, only 1 through 4 are printed.

In traditional switch statements, `break` is also commonly used to prevent execution from falling through into the next case.

The `continue` statement skips the remaining part of the current loop iteration and moves toward the next iteration.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) {
        continue;
    }
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

The value 3 is skipped, but the loop continues processing 4 and 5.

In a traditional `for` loop, the update expression still executes after `continue`. This matters because a continue statement should not accidentally prevent the loop variable from changing and create an infinite loop.

The `return` statement exits the current method. If the method has a return type, the statement provides the value that should be returned to the caller. A `void` method can use `return;` to exit early.

```java
static int calculate(int number) {
    if (number < 0) {
        return 0;
    }

    return number * 2;
}
```

When the first return executes, the method ends immediately. The remaining statements in that method are not executed.

Unlike `break` and `continue`, `return` is not limited to loops. It exits the entire current method.

A labeled `break` can terminate a specific enclosing loop instead of only the nearest loop. This can be useful when nested loops need to stop processing completely after a particular condition is reached.

```java
outer:
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
        if (i == 2 && j == 2) {
            break outer;
        }

        System.out.println(i + "," + j);
    }
}
```

Here, `break outer` terminates the loop marked with the `outer` label. Execution continues after that outer loop.

Labels are relatively uncommon in everyday Java code, but they can be useful when a nested-loop algorithm needs precise control over which loop should terminate.

A labeled `continue` skips to the next iteration of a specific labeled loop. This is different from an ordinary continue, which affects only the nearest enclosing loop.

```java
outer:
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
        if (j == 2) {
            continue outer;
        }

        System.out.println(i + "," + j);
    }
}
```

When `j` becomes 2, control moves directly to the next iteration of the outer loop. The remaining part of the inner loop is skipped for that outer iteration.

These three statements change control flow at different levels. `break` exits the nearest applicable loop or switch. `continue` skips the current iteration of a loop and proceeds toward the next iteration. `return` exits the entire current method.

For example, inside a loop:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 2) {
        continue;
    }

    if (i == 5) {
        break;
    }

    process(i);
}
```

The continue at 2 skips processing that iteration, while the break at 5 ends the loop completely. If the code used return instead, the method containing the loop would terminate rather than merely terminating the loop.

Encapsulation protects an object's internal state from uncontrolled modification. A class can keep its fields private and expose methods that enforce valid changes.

```java
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

Here, callers cannot directly assign an arbitrary value to `balance`. The `deposit()` method can enforce a rule before changing the state.

This gives the class control over its invariants and allows its internal representation to change without requiring every caller to know how the state is stored.

Abstraction focuses on what an object can do rather than exposing every detail of how it does it. An interface is a common way to define such a contract.

```java
interface PaymentMethod {
    void pay(double amount);
}

class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid by credit card: " + amount);
    }
}
```

Code using `PaymentMethod` does not need to know the internal implementation of `CreditCardPayment`. Another implementation such as `UPIPayment` can satisfy the same contract.

This separation reduces coupling and makes it easier to replace implementations without changing code that depends only on the abstraction.

Inheritance allows a class to extend another class and represent a subtype relationship.

```java
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}
```

A `Dog` object can use the inherited `eat()` method as well as its own `bark()` method.

Inheritance should represent a meaningful `is-a` relationship. A subclass should be usable where its superclass is expected without breaking the assumptions made by that code. Java allows a class to extend only one direct superclass, while a class can implement multiple interfaces.

Polymorphism allows a reference of a general type to refer to an object of a more specific type. When an instance method is overridden, Java uses dynamic dispatch to select the implementation based on the actual object at runtime.

```java
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}

Animal animal = new Dog();
animal.sound(); // Bark
```

The reference type is `Animal`, but the actual object is a `Dog`, so the overridden `Dog.sound()` implementation runs.

This is different from method overloading, where the compiler chooses a method based on the compile-time types and argument list.

Composition means building an object using other objects rather than extending a class. It often provides more flexibility because behavior can be changed by replacing collaborators instead of modifying a class hierarchy.

```java
class Engine {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Engine started");
    }
}

class Car {
    private final Engine engine;

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }

    void start() {
        engine.start();
    }
}
```

A `Car` has an `Engine`; it does not need to inherit from `Engine`. This is a `has-a` relationship rather than an `is-a` relationship.

Inheritance is appropriate when the subtype genuinely satisfies the parent contract. Composition is often preferable when one object simply needs to collaborate with another.

A class is a definition of a type that describes the fields and methods available to its objects. An object is a runtime instance of that class.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int salary;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name + " - " + salary);
    }
}

Employee employee = new Employee();
employee.name = "Rahul";
employee.salary = 50000;
employee.display();
```

The class describes what an `Employee` can contain and do. The object created with `new Employee()` represents one actual employee instance.

Multiple objects can be created from the same class, and each object has its own instance fields.

Instance fields belong to individual objects. If two objects are created from the same class, each object normally has its own copy of those fields.

```java
class Counter {
    int value;

    void increment() {
        value++;
    }
}

Counter first = new Counter();
Counter second = new Counter();

first.increment();

System.out.println(first.value);  // 1
System.out.println(second.value); // 0
```

The `increment()` method is defined once as part of the class, but when it is called, it operates on the particular object used as the receiver.

A variable whose type is a class normally stores a reference to an object rather than the object itself. Multiple reference variables can refer to the same object.

```java
Employee first = new Employee();
first.name = "Amit";

Employee second = first;
second.name = "Raj";

System.out.println(first.name); // Raj
```

Both `first` and `second` refer to the same object, so changing the object through one reference is visible through the other.

Assigning one object reference to another does not create a new object. A new object requires another object-creation operation such as `new Employee()`.

Every object has an identity that is distinct from the values stored in its fields. Two separate objects can contain exactly the same data but still be different objects.

```java
Employee first = new Employee();
Employee second = new Employee();

first.name = "Amit";
second.name = "Amit";

System.out.println(first == second); // false
```

The `==` operator compares references when used with objects, so it checks whether both references point to the same object. Logical equality is normally checked using `equals()` when the class provides an appropriate implementation.

A constructor is a special member used during object creation to initialize the new object's state. It has the same name as the class and does not have a return type, including `void`.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int salary;

    Employee(String name, int salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;
    }
}

Employee employee = new Employee("Amit", 50000);
```

The constructor receives the values required to establish the object's initial state. This is generally preferable to creating an object first and leaving it partially initialized for callers to configure later.

If a class declares no constructor, the compiler can provide a no-argument constructor automatically. This is commonly called the default constructor.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
}

Employee employee = new Employee();
```

However, once a class declares any constructor, the compiler no longer supplies that automatic constructor.

```java
class Employee {
    Employee(String name) {
        // initialization
    }
}

// Employee employee = new Employee(); // compile-time error
```

If callers still need a no-argument constructor, it must be declared explicitly.

A class can define multiple constructors with different parameter lists. This is constructor overloading.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int salary;

    Employee() {
        this("Unknown", 0);
    }

    Employee(String name) {
        this(name, 0);
    }

    Employee(String name, int salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;
    }
}
```

Overloaded constructors allow callers to create objects with different amounts of information while keeping initialization logic inside the class.

Using constructor chaining with `this(...)` can prevent duplicate initialization code.

The `this(...)` syntax invokes another constructor in the same class. When used, it must be the first statement in the constructor.

```java
class User {
    String name;
    int age;

    User() {
        this("Unknown", 0);
    }

    User(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}
```

The no-argument constructor delegates to the two-argument constructor. This centralizes initialization logic instead of repeating assignments in every constructor.

A constructor can use either `this(...)` or an explicit `super(...)` as its constructor invocation, but it cannot use both as separate first statements.

A subclass constructor can invoke a superclass constructor using `super(...)`.

```java
class Vehicle {
    String brand;

    Vehicle(String brand) {
        this.brand = brand;
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    int wheels;

    Car(String brand) {
        super(brand);
        this.wheels = 4;
    }
}
```

If a constructor does not explicitly invoke another constructor using `this(...)` or `super(...)`, Java attempts to insert a call to the superclass's no-argument constructor.

Therefore, if the superclass does not have an accessible no-argument constructor, the subclass must explicitly invoke one of the available superclass constructors.

Constructors are a useful place to enforce requirements that must always be true for an object.

```java
class Account {
    private final String accountNumber;

    Account(String accountNumber) {
        if (accountNumber == null || accountNumber.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Account number is required");
        }

        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
    }
}
```

After construction, an `Account` created successfully by this constructor has a non-empty account number. Establishing such invariants early prevents partially valid objects from spreading through the application.

Method overloading means defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists. The difference can be the number, type, or order of parameters.

```java
void print(int value) {
    System.out.println("int: " + value);
}

void print(double value) {
    System.out.println("double: " + value);
}

void print(String value) {
    System.out.println("String: " + value);
}
```

The return type alone cannot distinguish overloaded methods. Therefore, two methods with the same name and exactly the same parameter types cannot differ only by return type.

Overload selection happens at compile time. The compiler examines the arguments at the call site and determines which applicable method should be selected.

```java
void process(int value) {
    System.out.println("int");
}

void process(long value) {
    System.out.println("long");
}

process(10); // int
```

The literal `10` has type `int`, so the `int` overload is selected directly rather than widening it to `long`.

This differs from overriding, where an instance method implementation can be selected dynamically based on the actual object at runtime.

Java overload resolution considers different kinds of conversions. A method that can be invoked without certain conversions is generally preferred over one requiring additional conversions.

For example:

```java
void test(long value) {
    System.out.println("long");
}

void test(Integer value) {
    System.out.println("Integer");
}

test(10);
```

The `int` argument can widen to `long`, or it can be boxed to `Integer`. The compiler's overload-resolution rules determine which applicable conversion is preferred.

Varargs methods are considered when more direct applicable forms do not provide a better match. This is why adding overloads involving primitive types, wrappers, and varargs can sometimes change which method a call selects.

Passing `null` can make an overloaded call ambiguous when multiple unrelated reference-type overloads are applicable.

```java
void process(String value) {
}

void process(Integer value) {
}

// process(null); // compile-time error: ambiguous
```

`null` can be assigned to either `String` or `Integer`, and neither type is more specific than the other because they are unrelated reference types.

An explicit cast can resolve the ambiguity:

```java
process((String) null);
```

This is a common example used to demonstrate that overload resolution is performed by the compiler rather than dynamically at runtime.

Overloading and overriding are separate mechanisms. Overloading uses different parameter lists and is resolved at compile time. Overriding occurs when a subclass provides a compatible implementation of an inherited instance method, and the implementation is selected through dynamic dispatch at runtime.

```java
class Parent {
    void show(int value) {
        System.out.println("Parent int");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void show(int value) {
        System.out.println("Child int");
    }
}

Parent obj = new Child();
obj.show(10); // Child int
```

The method signature determines that `show(int)` is being called, while the actual object determines which overridden implementation executes.

A `static` member belongs to the class rather than to a particular object. A static field is shared by instances of that class, while a static method can be called without creating an instance.

```java
class Counter {
    static int count = 0;

    Counter() {
        count++;
    }
}

new Counter();
new Counter();

System.out.println(Counter.count); // 2
```

There is one class-level `count` value shared by the instances in this example. This is fundamentally different from an instance field, where every object has its own value.

A static method does not have an implicit `this` reference because it is invoked in a class context rather than as an operation on a particular object.

```java
class Calculator {
    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

int result = Calculator.add(10, 20);
```

A static method can directly access static members, but it cannot directly access an instance field or instance method.

```java
class Example {
    int value = 10;

    static void test() {
        // System.out.println(value); // compile-time error
    }
}
```

If instance state is required, an object reference must be available explicitly.

Static methods are useful when an operation does not depend on the state or identity of a particular object.

```java
class NumberUtils {
    static boolean isEven(int number) {
        return number % 2 == 0;
    }
}

boolean result = NumberUtils.isEven(10);
```

Java's standard library contains many examples of this style, such as `Math.max()` and `Integer.parseInt()`.

A utility method should generally be stateless. If a method needs significant object-specific state or dependencies, an instance-based design is often easier to test and reason about.

A mutable static field creates shared state that can be accessed by many objects and potentially by many threads.

```java
class Configuration {
    static String environment = "DEV";
}
```

Changing `Configuration.environment` changes the value observed by all code using that field. In an application, mutable static state can introduce hidden coupling and can make tests dependent on state left behind by earlier tests.

If shared state is genuinely required, it should be designed explicitly with appropriate lifecycle and concurrency considerations. Immutable static constants are generally easier to reason about.

Java supports static initialization blocks for initializing class-level state.

```java
class Config {
    static String environment;

    static {
        environment = "PRODUCTION";
    }
}
```

Static initialization is associated with class initialization rather than object construction. It happens when the class is initialized according to Java's class initialization rules.

Static initialization can be useful for class-level setup, but complex initialization logic can make startup behavior harder to understand. Prefer straightforward initialization when possible.

A `final` variable can be assigned only once. After it has been assigned, the variable cannot be reassigned.

```java
final int MAX = 100;

// MAX = 200; // compile-time error
```

A final variable can be initialized at its declaration or, for appropriate fields and local variables, during a valid initialization path. The important guarantee is that Java's definite-assignment rules prevent a final variable from being assigned more than once.

A final instance method cannot be overridden by a subclass.

```java
class Parent {
    final void display() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    // void display() {} // compile-time error
}
```

A final method can still be inherited and called by subclasses. The restriction is specifically that subclasses cannot provide another implementation through overriding.

This can be useful when a superclass needs to guarantee that particular behavior remains unchanged in subclasses.

A final class cannot be extended.

```java
final class Utility {
}

// class CustomUtility extends Utility {} // compile-time error
```

Declaring a class final prevents subclassing entirely. This can be useful when the class's design should not allow extension or when allowing subclasses could violate important assumptions.

For example, `String` is a final class, so another class cannot extend it and change its behavior.

A final instance field must be definitely assigned during object initialization. It can be assigned directly at declaration, in an initializer, or in a constructor depending on how the class is designed.

```java
class Employee {
    private final int id;

    Employee(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }
}
```

Here, each object receives its ID during construction and the field cannot later be reassigned. This is a common pattern for representing state that should remain fixed after an object is created.

A private member can be accessed only from within the class that declares it.

```java
class Account {
    private double balance;

    void deposit(double amount) {
        balance += amount;
    }
}
```

Code outside `Account` cannot directly access `balance`. This allows the class to control how the field changes.

Private access is one of the main tools used to implement encapsulation. Keeping implementation details private reduces the number of assumptions other classes can make about the class.

When no access modifier is specified for a class member, the member has package-private access. It can be accessed by other classes in the same package but not by unrelated classes in different packages.

```java
class Account {
    double balance;
}
```

Here, `balance` is package-private because no modifier is present.

Package-private access is useful when several classes form an internal implementation within the same package and do not need to expose those details as part of a broader public API.

Protected access has two important parts. A protected member is accessible to classes in the same package, and it also has special accessibility for subclasses, including subclasses in another package.

The cross-package subclass rule is more restrictive than simply saying that a protected member is public to subclasses. In a different package, access must occur through the appropriate subclass context rather than through an arbitrary superclass-typed reference.

```java
package pkgA;

public class Parent {
    protected void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}
```

A subclass in another package can access the protected member as part of its inherited subclass context, but code should not treat `protected` as unrestricted public access.

A public member has the broadest normal access and can be used by code outside its package, subject to other Java visibility boundaries such as modules and the accessibility of the enclosing type.

```java
public class UserService {
    public void createUser() {
        System.out.println("Creating user");
    }
}
```

Public methods are part of the API exposed by the class. Once other code depends on them, changing or removing them can have a larger compatibility impact than changing private implementation details.

A useful design approach is to start with the narrowest visibility that satisfies the requirement. Implementation details can remain private, package-level collaboration can remain package-private, and only behavior that genuinely needs broader access should become public.

```java
class User {
    private String name;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}
```

The class exposes a controlled operation instead of exposing the field directly. This gives the class more freedom to change how the value is stored or validated later.

`Exception` and `Error` both extend `Throwable`, but they represent different categories of problems.

Exceptions generally represent conditions that application code can potentially anticipate or handle. Errors generally indicate serious problems involving the JVM or runtime environment.

```java
try {
    riskyOperation();
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Handled application exception");
}
```

Catching every `Throwable` is usually inappropriate because it also catches serious `Error` conditions. Application code should normally catch the specific exception types it knows how to handle.

Java commonly divides exceptions into checked and unchecked categories.

Checked exceptions are exceptions other than `RuntimeException` subclasses and `Error` subclasses. The compiler requires checked exceptions that can escape a method to be either caught or declared with `throws`.

Unchecked exceptions include `RuntimeException` and its subclasses, as well as `Error` and its subclasses. The compiler does not require these to be caught or declared.

For example, `IOException` is checked:

```java
void readFile() throws IOException {
    // file operation
}
```

Whereas `NullPointerException` is unchecked and does not require a `throws` declaration.

The `try` block contains code that may throw an exception and therefore needs exception handling.

```java
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
    System.out.println(result);
}
```

A try block cannot normally stand alone. It must be followed by at least one `catch` block, a `finally` block, or both.

When an exception occurs inside the try block, normal execution of that block stops and Java searches for a compatible exception handler.

A `catch` block handles an exception thrown from its associated try block.

```java
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}
```

The exception parameter contains the thrown exception object. It can be used to inspect the exception, log relevant information, or perform an appropriate recovery action.

The catch type should normally be specific enough to represent the problem being handled. Catching a broad type such as `Exception` can hide programming errors if it is used without a clear reason.

A single try block can have multiple catch blocks when different exception types require different handling.

```java
try {
    processData();
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    System.out.println("Invalid number");
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("File operation failed");
}
```

Java checks catch clauses in order. A more specific exception type must appear before a broader type that could also catch it.

For example, placing `catch (Exception e)` before `catch (IOException e)` would make the IOException catch unreachable because `Exception` already catches it.

Java allows multiple unrelated exception types to be handled by one catch block using the multi-catch syntax.

```java
try {
    process();
} catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
    System.out.println("Operation failed: " + e.getMessage());
}
```

This is useful when different exception types require exactly the same handling. The alternatives in a multi-catch must not have a subtype relationship with each other.

The exception parameter in a multi-catch is implicitly final, so it cannot be reassigned inside the catch block.

The `finally` block contains code that is intended to execute when control leaves the associated try-catch structure, whether execution completes normally or an exception occurs.

```java
try {
    System.out.println("Processing");
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Error");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Cleanup");
}
```

Historically, finally has been used for cleanup such as closing resources. Modern Java generally prefers try-with-resources for objects implementing `AutoCloseable`, because it handles resource closing more reliably and with better exception-suppression behavior.

A `finally` block can be skipped in unusual situations such as abrupt JVM termination, so it should not be treated as an absolute guarantee that code will always execute.

The `throw` statement explicitly throws an exception object from the current execution point.

```java
void withdraw(double amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
    }
}
```

Once the throw statement executes, normal execution of the current block stops and Java searches for an appropriate handler. If no handler exists in the current method, the exception propagates to the caller.

The expression after `throw` must produce a reference to a `Throwable` object.

The `throws` clause declares that a method may allow one or more exceptions to propagate to its caller.

```java
void readFile(String path) throws IOException {
    Files.readString(Path.of(path));
}
```

The method is not necessarily throwing the exception at the moment the declaration is written. Instead, the declaration communicates that callers must account for the checked exception.

A caller can handle the exception with try-catch or declare it again so that it propagates further up the call chain.

`throw` and `throws` serve different purposes. `throw` is an executable statement used to actually throw an exception object. `throws` appears in a method declaration and communicates that the method can propagate specified exceptions.

```java
void validate(int age) throws IOException {
    if (age < 18) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age must be 18 or above");
    }
}
```

Here, `throw` performs the actual exception operation, while `throws` is part of the method's declaration.

If a method throws an exception and does not handle it, the exception can propagate to its caller. This continues up the call stack until a compatible catch block is found.

```java
void methodA() {
    methodB();
}

void methodB() {
    methodC();
}

void methodC() {
    throw new RuntimeException("Something failed");
}
```

The exception begins in `methodC()`. Since it is not handled there, it propagates through `methodB()` and then `methodA()`. If no appropriate handler is found, the exception eventually reaches the thread's uncaught-exception handling mechanism.

This propagation model is important when designing service layers because a low-level failure can be translated or handled at a higher layer where more context is available.

A checked custom exception can extend `Exception`.

```java
class InsufficientBalanceException extends Exception {
    public InsufficientBalanceException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

A method can then declare it:

```java
void withdraw(double amount) throws InsufficientBalanceException {
    if (amount > 1000) {
        throw new InsufficientBalanceException("Insufficient balance");
    }
}
```

Because the custom exception extends `Exception` rather than `RuntimeException`, callers must catch it or declare it.

A custom unchecked exception can extend `RuntimeException`.

```java
class InvalidOrderException extends RuntimeException {
    public InvalidOrderException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

It can be thrown without forcing callers to catch or declare it:

```java
if (order == null) {
    throw new InvalidOrderException("Order cannot be null");
}
```

Unchecked exceptions are often used for programming errors, invalid state, or conditions that callers cannot reasonably be expected to recover from at every call site.

Custom exceptions can provide constructors that preserve an underlying cause.

```java
class PaymentException extends RuntimeException {
    public PaymentException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
    }
}
```

The cause is useful when a lower-level exception needs to be translated into a domain-specific exception without losing the original failure.

```java
try {
    paymentGateway.charge();
} catch (IOException e) {
    throw new PaymentException("Payment processing failed", e);
}
```

The new exception communicates the business context while retaining the original exception for debugging and diagnosis.

A resource can be declared inside the try statement when it implements `AutoCloseable`.

```java
try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(Path.of("data.txt"))) {
    String line = reader.readLine();
    System.out.println(line);
}
```

When execution leaves the try block, Java automatically calls `close()` on the resource.

This works because `BufferedReader` implements `Closeable`, which extends `AutoCloseable`.

Multiple resources can be declared in the same try-with-resources statement.

```java
try (
    InputStream input = new FileInputStream("input.txt");
    OutputStream output = new FileOutputStream("output.txt")
) {
    input.transferTo(output);
}
```

Resources are closed automatically in the reverse order of their declaration. In this example, `output` is closed before `input`.

This ordering is useful when one resource depends on another and mirrors the natural stack-like structure of resource acquisition.

Any class that implements `AutoCloseable` can be used with try-with-resources.

```java
class MyResource implements AutoCloseable {
    void work() {
        System.out.println("Working");
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("Resource closed");
    }
}

try (MyResource resource = new MyResource()) {
    resource.work();
}
```

The compiler arranges for `close()` to be invoked when the try block is exited. This makes resource ownership explicit and greatly reduces the chance of forgetting cleanup.

A resource's `close()` method can itself throw an exception. If the main try block has already thrown an exception, the exception from `close()` is generally recorded as a suppressed exception rather than replacing the primary exception.

```java
try (MyResource resource = new MyResource()) {
    throw new RuntimeException("Main failure");
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println(e.getMessage());
    for (Throwable suppressed : e.getSuppressed()) {
        System.out.println(suppressed.getMessage());
    }
}
```

This behavior preserves both failures and makes try-with-resources safer than many manually written cleanup patterns.

An exception should generally be caught at a level where the application can make a meaningful decision about it. Catching an exception only to print a message and continue can hide the actual failure.

```java
try {
    processPayment();
} catch (PaymentException e) {
    showPaymentFailure(e.getMessage());
}
```

Here, the layer handling the exception has enough context to communicate the failure to the user or caller. If a lower-level method has no meaningful recovery action, allowing the exception to propagate may be more appropriate.

When translating one exception into another, preserve the original exception as the cause whenever it contains useful diagnostic information.

```java
try {
    repository.save(data);
} catch (SQLException e) {
    throw new DataAccessException("Unable to save data", e);
}
```

The new exception adds application-level context, while the original SQLException remains available through the exception cause.

Replacing an exception without preserving its cause can make production debugging much harder because the original failure information may be lost.

An empty catch block hides failures and makes troubleshooting difficult.

```java
try {
    process();
} catch (Exception e) {
    // Nothing happens
}
```

If the exception cannot be recovered from, the code should usually propagate it, translate it, or record appropriate diagnostic information. Ignoring an exception is sometimes intentional, but that decision should be explicit and justified by the application's behavior.

When an exception is logged, the original exception object should normally be supplied to the logging framework so that the stack trace and cause chain are preserved.

```java
try {
    processOrder();
} catch (OrderException e) {
    logger.error("Order processing failed", e);
}
```

Logging only `e.getMessage()` can lose valuable information such as the stack trace and nested cause. The amount and location of logging should also be controlled so that the same exception is not unnecessarily logged at every layer.
